[Music]  welcome back to science clique  today the mathematics of general  relativity  part 6 energy fluxes  our universe consists of a fabric  space-time  on which we place content stars  mass energy  at first glance the content of the  universe seems very diverse  there are stars planets interstellar gas  clouds  or even living beings like us  yet all this diversity can be summarized  with a single concept  movements of energy  energy is an abstract notion which  indicates the presence of disturbances  in the universe  particular it can arise from mass  mass contains energy according to the  formula e equals  m c squared but  other factors also contribute to the  energy content of the universe  such as the electromagnetic field or  even dark energy  which is known to have negative pressure  in all its forms energy moves through  space  space-time  similarly to a fluid  the presence of an apple for example can  be modeled as a flow of energy  to describe it let's consider a point on  the trajectory  from this point the motion of the energy  can be transferred in different  directions  on a two-dimensional sheet energy can  pass through four different sides  two along each coordinate  [Music]  however the situation is symmetrical if  energy enters the point from one side  it must leave through the opposite side  thus only two surfaces are needed to  fully describe the fluxes of energy  if the apple remains motionless through  space  its energy moves only through time  thus only the surface associated with  time will be crossed by the flow  and if the apple is moving through space  then both the time  and space surfaces will be crossed  for each surface let's represent the  flux of motion which  passes through it by a vector depending  on the behavior of energy  like a sort of fluid these vectors can  vary  breaking them down into their components  we can create a table which contains  these two vectors  this tensor describes how momentum is  transferred along the different  coordinates  we denote it capital t and call it the  energy momentum tensor  in this tensor the first component  measures the amount of energy travelling  through time  this component indicates how much energy  crosses the point  moving towards the future  it's more commonly called the energy  density  it gets greater when more energy passes  through the point  the next two components are equal both  measure how much  energy travels through space over time  this is momentum transport of spatial  motion  over time this value is greater when the  energy moves faster through space  these components are commonly called  momentum density  [Music]  finally the last component in the table  measures how much motion through space  is transmitted through space  this is more difficult to interpret  because we must find a meaning to this  transfer of motion through space  [Music]  this is a tendency that energy has to  push in one direction  it is commonly called pressure  in this two-dimensional example the  table contains  only four components two of which are  identical  but in our four-dimensional universe  this tensor actually contains  sixteen components  it contains the energy density the  amount of energy that progresses towards  the future  the momentum density which is this time  broken down into three components for  each direction through space  pressure which is also expressed along  the three directions  but also a bunch of other terms which  correspond  to viscosity viscosity is the tendency  that  energy has to transfer its motion to its  surroundings  it's important to understand that the  components of these fluxes of motion  depend on the coordinates that we choose  if we change coordinates a motionless  energy  can become a moving energy  in other words motion is relative it  depends on the point of view from which  it is observed  to illustrate this energy momentum  tensor we will look at two standard  examples  the first example is that of an empty  space time  when we calculate it at a point which is  crossed by no  energy the energy momentum tensor is  zero  all its components vanish  this very simple case is fundamental  because it is the one that we will use  most often  indeed when we want to describe the  motion of a small object through  space-time  for example a satellite around earth or  an apple near a black hole  we can neglect the energy of this small  object which is very weak  compared to that of the central body we  can consider that the object is located  in empty space  and the energy momentum tensor vanishes  [Music]  the second concrete example is the case  in which we are interested  in the inside of a body for example  we may want to describe how an object  behaves inside the sun  in this case even if we neglect the mass  of the small object  it is still not in a vacuum because we  have to account for the matter that  makes up the sun  in such a case the energy momentum  tensor is not zero  if we consider that the sun is formed by  a perfect fluid  without viscosity and at equilibrium  then the tensor contains only an energy  density  related to the mass of the sun and the  pressure  directed outwards which prevents it from  collapsing in on itself  [Music]  [Music]  you

